1
00:00:00,970 --> 00:00:04,600
♪ ♪

2
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Tonight...

3
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:19,300
&gt;&gt; By noon we had conceded
that the town

4
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:20,830
had basically burned down.

5
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:23,770
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: One year since
California's deadliest fire...

6
00:00:23,770 --> 00:00:26,800
&gt;&gt; The plan was completely
overwhelmed by circumstances.

7
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:31,030
But I think those circumstances
were not unprecedented.

8
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:33,170
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: ..."Frontline"
takes you inside that day...

9
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:35,700
&gt;&gt; The road's completely
engulfed in flames.

10
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,730
And I told my husband, I'm like,
"I can't run through fire."

11
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:40,630
And he said,
"You're going to have to."

12
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:43,900
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: ...exposing the new
dangers of a changing climate.

13
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:46,700
&gt;&gt; We just did not anticipate
a fire that went

14
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:47,930
seven-and-a-half miles
in an hour and a half.

15
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:49,900
I don't think anybody envisioned
that happening.

16
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:52,400
&gt;&gt; Do you think you should have
envisioned that happening?

17
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:53,930
&gt;&gt; I'm not going to answer
that question.

18
00:00:53,930 --> 00:00:56,400
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: And a giant power
company under scrutiny.

19
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:01,330
&gt;&gt; Is what PG&amp;E did or did not
do grossly negligent?

20
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:03,700
&gt;&gt; They've been on probation,
they've violated the probation.

21
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,570
If PG&amp;E was an individual
and not a corporation,

22
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:09,470
I think by now they would be
in prison.

23
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,730
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Tonight on
"Frontline,"

24
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:13,670
"Fire in Paradise."

25
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:20,570
♪ ♪

26
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,070
&gt;&gt; Paradise is...

27
00:01:52,070 --> 00:01:54,570
There's something about it,
there's something

28
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:56,230
with the country that's...

29
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:57,570
The trees are beautiful.

30
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:04,200
Just living in the mountains,
and...

31
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:06,000
It's healing to be here.

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,670
&gt;&gt; You saw hummingbirds and
butterflies.

33
00:02:08,670 --> 00:02:13,530
We'd sleep outside
under the stars.

34
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:14,870
It's a tight-knit community.

35
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:20,300
Everyone is super-strong
and resilient up here.

36
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:23,530
You never felt more safe than
out there in the mountains.

37
00:02:23,530 --> 00:02:25,670
♪ ♪

38
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:34,900
&gt;&gt; Good morning and it's...

39
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:37,630
A red-flag fire danger warning
is in effect.

40
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:41,230
Up to 45-mile-per-hour gusts out
of the north today.

41
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:43,200
Right now, it's 57 degrees.

42
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,070
Humidity down to 19% already...

43
00:02:45,070 --> 00:02:47,200
♪ ♪

44
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:49,670
&gt;&gt; I woke up early the morning
of the eighth.

45
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:51,970
The wind was very strong.

46
00:02:51,970 --> 00:02:53,800
Pine needles were hitting
the roof.

47
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:57,100
It's a metal roof,
and in my half-asleep state,

48
00:02:57,100 --> 00:02:58,830
I thought, "Is it raining?"

49
00:02:58,830 --> 00:03:02,630
Any time you have the winds
coming with no rain,

50
00:03:02,630 --> 00:03:04,930
it's very nerve-wracking.

51
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:07,000
And we were getting so late
in the season,

52
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,500
we were just critically dry.

53
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:14,430
It was just like, please,
blow in a storm.

54
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:16,970
You know, every now and then,
I like to wake up early

55
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:18,830
and make the guys breakfast.

56
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:20,870
So when the wind woke me up,
I said,

57
00:03:20,870 --> 00:03:22,570
"Well, this is a perfect time
to get a jump on it."

58
00:03:22,570 --> 00:03:25,730
My phone was laying
on the countertop

59
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,830
next to where I was cutting up
potatoes, and it illuminated.

60
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:31,170
Said there was a vegetation fire
in the canyon.

61
00:03:34,330 --> 00:03:36,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Seven-and-a-half
miles from the town of Paradise,

62
00:03:36,570 --> 00:03:40,300
a fire had started beneath a
high-voltage electricity tower.

63
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:48,330
The line was almost 100 years
old and was owned by PG&amp;E,

64
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:51,900
America's largest
electricity company.

65
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,130
&gt;&gt; The fire started,
as PG&amp;E has admitted,

66
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:57,930
from a piece of equipment
that failed,

67
00:03:57,930 --> 00:04:03,770
bringing a power line in contact
with the steel tower,

68
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:08,630
so you had shards of molten
metal that got thrown down

69
00:04:08,630 --> 00:04:10,700
into the brush.

70
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:11,900
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: In high winds,

71
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,170
companies like PG&amp;E can turn off
the electricity

72
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:18,000
in power lines
to reduce wildfire risk.

73
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,830
&gt;&gt; We had heard that PG&amp;E was
thinking about turning off power

74
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:25,830
in, in several different areas
that were in danger

75
00:04:25,830 --> 00:04:28,100
of high winds and possibly
something happening

76
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:29,700
with power lines.

77
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:31,500
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: But that morning,

78
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:34,670
PG&amp;E had decided not to turn off
the power.

79
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:36,400
It would later say this was
because the winds

80
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,830
were decreasing.

81
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:41,270
&gt;&gt; I made one corner
around Highway 70

82
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:43,270
to where you can actually
see the Pulga Bridge.

83
00:04:43,270 --> 00:04:46,330
And so I took my eyes off the
road for two seconds, looked up,

84
00:04:46,330 --> 00:04:49,100
saw it and made my report.

85
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,830
(radio static hissing)

86
00:04:52,830 --> 00:04:56,430
(people talking on radio)

87
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:10,400
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The fire was
by a narrow dirt track

88
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,530
called Camp Creek Road.

89
00:05:12,530 --> 00:05:15,130
Captain McKenzie decided it was
too dangerous

90
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:17,370
to drive a fire truck down it.

91
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,330
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: He requested air
support to put out the fire,

92
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:26,530
but it was too windy to fly.

93
00:05:32,770 --> 00:05:35,670
♪ ♪

94
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,770
It was a very sinking,
very uncomfortable feeling

95
00:05:39,770 --> 00:05:44,100
seeing where it was at, um,

96
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,630
and seeing how small
it actually was

97
00:05:45,630 --> 00:05:47,230
relative to where it was at.

98
00:05:47,230 --> 00:05:51,330
It was a manageable-looking
fire, if I could get to it.

99
00:05:51,330 --> 00:05:53,570
So...
&gt;&gt; But you couldn't get to it.

100
00:05:53,570 --> 00:05:56,070
&gt;&gt; Couldn't get to it.

101
00:05:56,070 --> 00:05:59,730
(radio static hissing)

102
00:05:59,730 --> 00:06:02,330
(woman talking on radio)

103
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,130
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The fire was
spreading towards Concow,

104
00:06:33,130 --> 00:06:35,330
a remote settlement of around
700 people,

105
00:06:35,330 --> 00:06:39,870
about halfway between where
the fire ignited and Paradise.

106
00:06:39,870 --> 00:06:42,630
&gt;&gt; I got a couple of phone calls
from other chief officers asking

107
00:06:42,630 --> 00:06:45,200
if I was paying attention
to the radio.

108
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,870
You know, I think like
a lot of people,

109
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:49,730
didn't really take it too
serious--

110
00:06:49,730 --> 00:06:51,600
we get a lot of fires up there.

111
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:53,600
You know, I told them, you know,
it's cold, you know,

112
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:58,270
it's in the 40s, it's November,
it's a nuisance fire.

113
00:06:58,270 --> 00:07:00,200
The incident command post was
set up

114
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:02,530
at the hardware store
at Yankee Hill.

115
00:07:02,530 --> 00:07:04,470
And so we were preparing to
defend Concow

116
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:07,000
and contain that fire.

117
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,270
(woman talking on radio)

118
00:07:10,270 --> 00:07:12,400
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

119
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:13,530
&gt;&gt; ...21-07...

120
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:25,030
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Cal Fire--
the state fire service--

121
00:07:25,030 --> 00:07:29,770
began sending firefighters to
tackle the blaze in Concow.

122
00:07:29,770 --> 00:07:33,430
&gt;&gt; I drove up Highway 70 and the
wind was basically blowing

123
00:07:33,430 --> 00:07:35,130
all the smoke right over
the top of us.

124
00:07:37,670 --> 00:07:39,800
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The blaze was soon
dubbed "The Camp Fire,"

125
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,900
after the road where it started.

126
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:44,470
&gt;&gt; We were stopping down Concow,

127
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:46,100
helped out a few residents,

128
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:48,530
tried to put some of the spot
fires out around their house.

129
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:53,170
They were relatively small, they
were ten to 15, maybe 20 feet.

130
00:07:53,170 --> 00:07:55,070
And then there was a point in
there where the wind just kind

131
00:07:55,070 --> 00:07:59,830
of started picking up, and the
spot fires that were not

132
00:07:59,830 --> 00:08:02,930
a big deal at the time started
engulfing both sides

133
00:08:02,930 --> 00:08:04,800
of the road.

134
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:08,770
♪ ♪

135
00:08:16,630 --> 00:08:20,700
&gt;&gt; My Pops had been in Concow
ever since I can remember,

136
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:23,500
before I was born.

137
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:24,500
It's always felt so special.

138
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,200
It's at the end of Concow Road.

139
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,830
And, like, at the top,
we always felt

140
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:29,530
like nothing could hurt us
there.

141
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:32,300
And it was home sweet home.

142
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:35,470
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: 21-year-old Jordan
Huff often visited her granddad,

143
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:39,530
who lived on his own
on a small farm.

144
00:08:39,530 --> 00:08:41,600
&gt;&gt; He'd grow pumpkins
for the grandkids.

145
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:43,470
So in October, when they were
ready to harvest,

146
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:45,100
we'd have jack-o'-lanterns
to carve.

147
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,900
And they were Poppa's pumpkins

148
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:49,600
and they were bigger than
anyone's you'd seen.

149
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:52,630
My Pops lost his leg in a
farming incident,

150
00:08:52,630 --> 00:08:55,400
but they're the stubborn
mountain folk.

151
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:57,600
He was always outside working
when we showed up,

152
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,930
out in his wheelchair
working away.

153
00:09:00,930 --> 00:09:02,900
♪ ♪

154
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:13,200
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: By 7:30,
the fire had picked up.

155
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:16,800
The wind was spraying burning
embers in every direction.

156
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:20,500
A column of smoke was now
visible for miles.

157
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:22,270
&gt;&gt; My dad had called my Pops.

158
00:09:22,270 --> 00:09:25,400
He was out there in his
wheelchair, um, with a hose,

159
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:29,530
um, putting out the fires that
were breaking out into his yard,

160
00:09:29,530 --> 00:09:31,930
and my dad was, like, you know,
"Don't worry about it,

161
00:09:31,930 --> 00:09:33,230
you need to go.

162
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:35,170
You need to get out of here and
leave."

163
00:09:35,170 --> 00:09:36,270
And he said, "Okay, I will.

164
00:09:36,270 --> 00:09:39,500
I'll grab the dogs and I'll go."

165
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,700
♪ ♪

166
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:43,030
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Firefighter Jeff
Edson and a colleague

167
00:09:43,030 --> 00:09:46,500
were now trapped
down by Concow Lake.

168
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,700
&gt;&gt; We came across four
individuals that were running,

169
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:50,100
and they were waving
their hands at me,

170
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:52,230
and you could tell they had
ember burns and stuff

171
00:09:52,230 --> 00:09:53,970
on their skin and their hair.

172
00:09:53,970 --> 00:09:56,500
Three of them ran and just
jumped straight in the water,

173
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:59,370
'cause they were taking
so much heat.

174
00:09:59,370 --> 00:10:02,700
♪ ♪

175
00:10:14,530 --> 00:10:16,130
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: At the incident
command post,

176
00:10:16,130 --> 00:10:19,130
Chief Messina was aware
this was becoming a major fire.

177
00:10:19,130 --> 00:10:21,770
&gt;&gt; Just be ready to call in
personnel

178
00:10:21,770 --> 00:10:23,570
that are off-duty right now.

179
00:10:23,570 --> 00:10:26,030
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: But with
firefighters in Concow trapped,

180
00:10:26,030 --> 00:10:28,600
and aircraft unable to fly
because of the wind,

181
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:32,170
he didn't know how fast
it was moving.

182
00:10:32,170 --> 00:10:34,900
&gt;&gt; We typically get our fire
intelligence,

183
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:38,030
what the fire's doing,
how fast it's spreading,

184
00:10:38,030 --> 00:10:40,330
from our own line personnel.

185
00:10:40,330 --> 00:10:42,070
Um... firefighters.

186
00:10:42,070 --> 00:10:44,070
What was different
about this day

187
00:10:44,070 --> 00:10:47,130
was the fact that as soon
as our firefighters engaged,

188
00:10:47,130 --> 00:10:48,700
they went right into
rescue mode.

189
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:52,500
And they, they were no longer
able, nor did they really care,

190
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,600
where the fire was spreading.

191
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:58,300
They were too busy on rescuing
civilians, and, you know,

192
00:10:58,300 --> 00:11:01,300
ensuring that...
of their own safety.

193
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:03,130
So we didn't get a lot
of intelligence

194
00:11:03,130 --> 00:11:05,000
on how fast the fire
was spreading.

195
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,330
♪ ♪

196
00:11:08,330 --> 00:11:10,530
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The fire was moving
towards the town of Paradise,

197
00:11:10,530 --> 00:11:13,570
four miles away on the other
side of a steep canyon.

198
00:11:16,970 --> 00:11:21,670
In the past, fires have rarely
crossed the canyon,

199
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:23,330
but the Camp Fire was now
spreading at a rate

200
00:11:23,330 --> 00:11:25,600
of 80 football fields a minute.

201
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:27,630
(telephone ringing)

202
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:37,330
&gt;&gt; The calls started coming in
slowly as people were waking up

203
00:11:37,330 --> 00:11:40,230
in the morning, having their
coffee, looking out the window,

204
00:11:40,230 --> 00:11:42,600
and seeing what I couldn't see.

205
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:43,900
(telephone ringing)

206
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:46,200
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Dispatcher Carol
Ladrini had been trained

207
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:48,200
to handle calls reporting fires.

208
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:49,600
&gt;&gt; Do you see ashes?

209
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:51,770
Do you see flames?

210
00:11:51,770 --> 00:11:53,470
How close is it?

211
00:11:53,470 --> 00:11:56,130
Because kind of far off could be
across the street

212
00:11:56,130 --> 00:11:58,130
or two canyons away.

213
00:11:58,130 --> 00:12:01,500
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Cal Fire normally
notifies Paradise Police

214
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:05,370
if a fire is threatening the
town, but they hadn't done so.

215
00:12:15,470 --> 00:12:17,270
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR:
As more calls came in,

216
00:12:17,270 --> 00:12:20,130
Ladrini says she contacted
Cal Fire,

217
00:12:20,130 --> 00:12:22,500
and they told her the fire
was north of Concow--

218
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:25,270
miles from Paradise.

219
00:12:25,270 --> 00:12:27,830
&gt;&gt; Did they say anything about
the size

220
00:12:27,830 --> 00:12:30,270
or the intensity of the fire?
&gt;&gt; No.

221
00:12:30,270 --> 00:12:33,170
At that point, they didn't,
and, and I didn't ask.

222
00:12:33,170 --> 00:12:36,200
Generally, a fire that far away

223
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:38,900
would never even get close
to Paradise.

224
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:40,770
&gt;&gt; Paradise Police.

225
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:53,170
&gt;&gt; Why are so many people
calling about this smoke?

226
00:12:53,170 --> 00:12:55,030
What... what's going on?

227
00:12:55,030 --> 00:12:59,470
Still, at that point, I didn't
know what they were seeing.

228
00:12:59,470 --> 00:13:00,730
(telephone ringing)

229
00:13:00,730 --> 00:13:04,370
So all I could do was call
Cal Fire back.

230
00:13:04,370 --> 00:13:08,330
What I said was,
"Can you confirm with me

231
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:12,270
that this is north of Concow,
that this is not in Paradise?

232
00:13:12,270 --> 00:13:14,170
People say there's ashes
falling."

233
00:13:14,170 --> 00:13:17,000
"Yes, it's north of Concow."

234
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,470
That's the words that I got.

235
00:13:18,470 --> 00:13:19,970
"Okay."

236
00:13:19,970 --> 00:13:22,670
So I continued to tell the
people that were calling

237
00:13:22,670 --> 00:13:24,670
that we were not under threat.

238
00:13:24,670 --> 00:13:26,600
(telephone ringing)

239
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:48,270
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: By 7:45,
the fire had crossed the canyon

240
00:13:48,270 --> 00:13:50,900
and was threatening Paradise
and the surrounding area,

241
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:53,500
home to 40,000 people.

242
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:56,400
Cal Fire issued an evacuation
order for residents

243
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:59,270
on the east side of Paradise,
but not for those

244
00:13:59,270 --> 00:14:00,870
from other parts of town.

245
00:14:15,030 --> 00:14:16,970
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: 18 minutes after
fire entered the town,

246
00:14:16,970 --> 00:14:20,000
Carol Ladrini received a call
from Cal Fire.

247
00:14:39,830 --> 00:14:43,400
&gt;&gt; When I started as a
firefighter in the mid-1980s,

248
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:44,770
we had large fires.

249
00:14:44,770 --> 00:14:46,170
You know, it wasn't uncommon.

250
00:14:46,170 --> 00:14:48,300
And we may be at a large fire
for a week or two,

251
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:50,130
maybe even a little bit longer.

252
00:14:50,130 --> 00:14:51,870
But then the periods would
subside and we would,

253
00:14:51,870 --> 00:14:53,430
we would go back,
we'd regroup,

254
00:14:53,430 --> 00:14:55,530
and we'd get ready for the next
round.

255
00:14:55,530 --> 00:14:57,700
Now, in the current fire
environment,

256
00:14:57,700 --> 00:14:59,070
the season is much longer.

257
00:14:59,070 --> 00:15:02,500
The summer is much hotter,
drier, less humidity,

258
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:04,930
and typically,
our winters have been

259
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:08,170
on the lower end of average.

260
00:15:08,170 --> 00:15:12,330
&gt;&gt; We measure climate at weather
stations, and when fires burn,

261
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:15,030
we trace their footprint.

262
00:15:15,030 --> 00:15:18,230
Those types of analyses
have shown

263
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:22,200
that human-caused climate change
has doubled wildfire

264
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:25,670
since 1984 across
the western United States

265
00:15:25,670 --> 00:15:29,530
above what would have burned
without climate change.

266
00:15:29,530 --> 00:15:31,800
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Researchers say
that in Northern California,

267
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,970
summers have warmed
by an average of 2.5 degrees

268
00:15:34,970 --> 00:15:37,200
in the last 50 years.

269
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:40,730
At the same time, climate change
has made prolonged drought

270
00:15:40,730 --> 00:15:43,600
more common in the area.

271
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:46,500
&gt;&gt; What we've observed over the
last several fire seasons

272
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:49,330
is that it doesn't rain until
late in December

273
00:15:49,330 --> 00:15:54,370
or even early January, and
that means that the landscape

274
00:15:54,370 --> 00:15:56,900
hasn't seen a drop of
precipitation

275
00:15:56,900 --> 00:16:00,070
in perhaps eight months.

276
00:16:00,070 --> 00:16:02,570
It's that combination
of factors,

277
00:16:02,570 --> 00:16:05,230
where you get the high winds,
you get the high temperatures,

278
00:16:05,230 --> 00:16:08,270
you have fuels
that are bone-dry,

279
00:16:08,270 --> 00:16:12,100
and you combine all of those
factors into a package

280
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:15,870
that is really explosive
from a wildland perspective,

281
00:16:15,870 --> 00:16:18,830
where then, if you throw
a match into that package,

282
00:16:18,830 --> 00:16:21,430
you're going to generate
a catastrophe.

283
00:16:21,430 --> 00:16:24,030
(radio static hissing)

284
00:16:24,030 --> 00:16:27,470
&gt;&gt; All units be advised,
the town of Paradise is under

285
00:16:27,470 --> 00:16:29,270
a mandatory evacuation.

286
00:16:29,270 --> 00:16:32,400
The town of Paradise is under
a mandatory evacuation.

287
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:35,330
(man breathing heavily)

288
00:16:35,330 --> 00:16:40,000
&gt;&gt; I was dispatched to the fire
down on the east canyon edge.

289
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,870
So I slid my body camera on and
went behind the house.

290
00:16:44,870 --> 00:16:50,200
I can hear a roaring, and I
could see flames coming up from,

291
00:16:50,200 --> 00:16:53,230
from the canyon that were
probably 30, 40 feet in height.

292
00:16:53,230 --> 00:16:54,170
(Pickering speaking on radio)

293
00:16:59,370 --> 00:17:00,630
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Fire was now
established

294
00:17:00,630 --> 00:17:02,930
on the east side of Paradise.

295
00:17:02,930 --> 00:17:05,570
Police went door-to-door to make
sure people had left.

296
00:17:14,570 --> 00:17:17,300
&gt;&gt; The fire was swirling around
the houses.

297
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:19,230
It was coming in at all angles,

298
00:17:19,230 --> 00:17:22,470
defying any sense of gravity or
any sense of, in my mind, what

299
00:17:22,470 --> 00:17:24,400
would be normal for a fire.

300
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:26,370
Too much was happening,
too much was going on,

301
00:17:26,370 --> 00:17:28,030
and we were not able
to do more than

302
00:17:28,030 --> 00:17:29,500
just a couple of handful
of streets.

303
00:17:35,770 --> 00:17:37,200
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Sergeant Pickering
made his way

304
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:41,900
to Paradise's largest building,
Feather River Hospital.

305
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:45,300
&gt;&gt; My husband texts me, and he
says, "Hey, there's a big fire."

306
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:47,130
And I said, "Huh."

307
00:17:47,130 --> 00:17:48,300
I said, "I didn't see anything.

308
00:17:48,300 --> 00:17:49,800
Where's it coming from?"

309
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:51,300
He goes, "Out of Concow."

310
00:17:51,300 --> 00:17:53,770
And I said, "Okay, well,

311
00:17:53,770 --> 00:17:55,730
hopefully it doesn't cross
the canyon,

312
00:17:55,730 --> 00:17:57,730
'cause then I'm gonna have
to evacuate the hospital."

313
00:17:57,730 --> 00:18:03,100
Um... and then we saw
the orange glow

314
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:05,800
through the patients' rooms.

315
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:10,830
♪ ♪

316
00:18:10,830 --> 00:18:11,770
&gt;&gt; For the moment, yes.

317
00:18:20,630 --> 00:18:22,670
&gt;&gt; There was people that were
having to carry an I.V. bag

318
00:18:22,670 --> 00:18:24,570
with them, they were holding
their own I.V. bag.

319
00:18:24,570 --> 00:18:27,270
And then we had people that were
just coming out of surgery

320
00:18:27,270 --> 00:18:29,130
that had to be loaded up.

321
00:18:37,570 --> 00:18:40,000
&gt;&gt; Doctors pulled up
with their SUVs

322
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,070
and were putting patients in
with doctors.

323
00:18:44,430 --> 00:18:46,270
&gt;&gt; Okay, hang a hard right...

324
00:18:46,270 --> 00:18:48,770
&gt;&gt; And nurses are driving their
own private vehicles

325
00:18:48,770 --> 00:18:51,000
and taking out their car seats

326
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,630
and leaving them on the side
of the hospital ground.

327
00:18:53,630 --> 00:18:57,430
It wasn't a normal evacuation
that we've been planning and,

328
00:18:57,430 --> 00:18:59,830
and rehearsing, it was so fast.

329
00:18:59,830 --> 00:19:01,600
&gt;&gt; What was that?

330
00:19:04,030 --> 00:19:07,400
&gt;&gt; Anywhere from a few minutes
to 15, 20 minutes,

331
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:09,900
everything around the hospital
was burning and on fire.

332
00:19:09,900 --> 00:19:11,400
It went black real quick.

333
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:14,570
It felt like, it felt like
working a night shift.

334
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:33,700
(radio squawking)

335
00:19:44,530 --> 00:19:46,970
(Pickering sighs)

336
00:19:46,970 --> 00:19:52,200
♪ ♪

337
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:56,270
&gt;&gt; We were stuck in traffic for
quite a while in the hospital

338
00:19:56,270 --> 00:19:57,670
as everything around us
is on fire.

339
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:10,200
&gt;&gt; Well, where's the fire
department, where's the hoses?

340
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:12,800
Why isn't anybody putting these
fires out?

341
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:15,000
You know, it was so confusing.

342
00:20:29,430 --> 00:20:33,870
&gt;&gt; I assumed that the fire was
right there, next to me.

343
00:20:33,870 --> 00:20:37,400
I didn't know, at the time, that
the fire had jumped

344
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:39,600
all the way into Paradise.

345
00:20:39,600 --> 00:20:41,800
Nobody said anything to us.

346
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:45,500
Nobody said, "Hey, all of
Paradise is on fire."

347
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:50,570
(people speaking on radio)

348
00:20:50,570 --> 00:20:54,930
&gt;&gt; Copy, 0922.

349
00:20:54,930 --> 00:20:57,100
&gt;&gt; Multiple structures on fire
here...

350
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:01,230
Start towards Paradise.

351
00:21:01,230 --> 00:21:03,030
&gt;&gt; See the fire's about to jump
the road.

352
00:21:03,030 --> 00:21:05,200
(people talking on radio)

353
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:06,970
&gt;&gt; Picture it like
a snow blizzard.

354
00:21:06,970 --> 00:21:09,470
There was just thousands upon
thousands of embers

355
00:21:09,470 --> 00:21:11,600
blowing through the air.

356
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:14,870
It was really hard
to get your mind around

357
00:21:14,870 --> 00:21:17,770
how rapidly it was developing.

358
00:21:17,770 --> 00:21:19,300
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR:
In less than an hour,

359
00:21:19,300 --> 00:21:22,670
the fire swept across the town
of Paradise,

360
00:21:22,670 --> 00:21:25,400
overwhelming the firefighters'
efforts to stop it.

361
00:21:25,400 --> 00:21:27,070
&gt;&gt; The homes, the homes are
becoming involved.

362
00:21:27,070 --> 00:21:29,370
(people talking on radio)

363
00:21:29,370 --> 00:21:31,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The smoke, swirling
with burning pine needles

364
00:21:31,570 --> 00:21:34,930
and pieces of houses,
turned day to night.

365
00:21:34,930 --> 00:21:36,530
&gt;&gt; An area would catch on fire,

366
00:21:36,530 --> 00:21:39,000
homes would catch on fire
generating heat,

367
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:42,070
which would throw more embers,
that would start another fire.

368
00:21:42,070 --> 00:21:45,470
And those winds can push those
embers a long ways.

369
00:21:45,470 --> 00:21:50,100
And it just kind of perpetuates
into one big fire at once.

370
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:52,530
There was no, there was no
flaming front.

371
00:21:52,530 --> 00:21:56,170
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: In a typical fire,
the smoke travels straight up,

372
00:21:56,170 --> 00:22:00,100
where cooler air puts out most
of the embers.

373
00:22:00,100 --> 00:22:03,530
But in this fire, winds high up
of up to 100 miles an hour

374
00:22:03,530 --> 00:22:06,570
were blowing
the embers sideways.

375
00:22:06,570 --> 00:22:10,070
&gt;&gt; The wind aloft
that lofted the embers

376
00:22:10,070 --> 00:22:13,000
was a lot stronger wind
than the wind at the surface.

377
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:14,770
And that's what allowed it to...

378
00:22:14,770 --> 00:22:15,870
(imitates explosion)

379
00:22:15,870 --> 00:22:19,270
...throw fireballs
all over our town.

380
00:22:22,270 --> 00:22:23,870
I think that's what
differentiates this fire

381
00:22:23,870 --> 00:22:25,600
from the other fires.

382
00:22:25,600 --> 00:22:28,800
That they all had a path,
and this one didn't.

383
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:29,870
It really didn't.

384
00:22:29,870 --> 00:22:31,330
It had paths.

385
00:22:31,330 --> 00:22:35,000
It had a lot of paths, um,
and they were all happening

386
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,430
at the same time.

387
00:22:38,430 --> 00:22:39,470
&gt;&gt; Oh, my God!

388
00:22:39,470 --> 00:22:43,200
♪ ♪

389
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:47,100
&gt;&gt; There was, like, no sirens or
warnings or anything.

390
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:49,930
No one telling anyone for sure
what was happening.

391
00:22:49,930 --> 00:22:52,100
So we're, like,
"Oh, let's go check it out."

392
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:53,830
(horn honking)

393
00:22:53,830 --> 00:22:55,930
We just get in the car and we
can't even pull out,

394
00:22:55,930 --> 00:22:59,230
'cause there was cars
all the way down.

395
00:22:59,230 --> 00:23:01,400
You couldn't even get
on the road.

396
00:23:01,400 --> 00:23:02,930
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Jordan Huff was
trying to leave

397
00:23:02,930 --> 00:23:06,430
with her boyfriend along
Paradise's main road, Skyway.

398
00:23:08,830 --> 00:23:10,500
&gt;&gt; Everything was red,

399
00:23:10,500 --> 00:23:11,830
everything just seemed
like panic.

400
00:23:15,830 --> 00:23:17,470
&gt;&gt; I started freaking out,

401
00:23:17,470 --> 00:23:20,130
because the fire's coming at us
and I didn't wanna see it,

402
00:23:20,130 --> 00:23:21,870
I didn't wanna feel it.

403
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:23,270
Like, I didn't wanna be there.

404
00:23:23,270 --> 00:23:25,030
I just kinda wanted
to disappear,

405
00:23:25,030 --> 00:23:27,430
because I couldn't believe
this was happening.

406
00:23:27,430 --> 00:23:30,500
♪ ♪

407
00:23:30,500 --> 00:23:31,700
&gt;&gt; Holy (bleep)!

408
00:23:31,700 --> 00:23:33,970
&gt;&gt; It was suffering,
moving that slow.

409
00:23:33,970 --> 00:23:37,500
I didn't understand why not
everyone was flooring it.

410
00:23:37,500 --> 00:23:40,030
Like, we were all about
to burn alive.

411
00:23:40,030 --> 00:23:42,800
Like, why isn't everyone, like,
full speed ahead?

412
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:44,130
Like, why are we stuck?

413
00:23:44,130 --> 00:23:45,230
Like, why?

414
00:23:45,230 --> 00:23:46,270
How?

415
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:56,100
&gt;&gt; The town of Paradise
and the upper ridge

416
00:23:56,100 --> 00:24:00,100
has had a community evacuation
plan since the late '90s.

417
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:04,370
In the early 2000s, that plan
was updated and included maps

418
00:24:04,370 --> 00:24:06,900
with zones in them.

419
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:10,100
Paradise is limited by the
number of routes out of town.

420
00:24:10,100 --> 00:24:11,500
Each fire is different,
you know.

421
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:13,130
Fires come from
different directions.

422
00:24:13,130 --> 00:24:15,000
So we had to look at varying
scenarios

423
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,770
and determine what intersections
would need controlling

424
00:24:17,770 --> 00:24:22,000
under a normally
developing fire.

425
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,200
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The emergency
planners had divided the town

426
00:24:24,200 --> 00:24:26,100
into 14 zones.

427
00:24:26,100 --> 00:24:27,970
They would be evacuated in turn

428
00:24:27,970 --> 00:24:30,900
depending on where the fire
came from.

429
00:24:30,900 --> 00:24:33,570
&gt;&gt; We actually had a trial run
in 2008.

430
00:24:33,570 --> 00:24:36,330
We evacuated the zones on the
east side of town

431
00:24:36,330 --> 00:24:37,770
for a fire coming from Concow.

432
00:24:37,770 --> 00:24:42,500
The whole lesson learned from
2008 was, the more you evacuate,

433
00:24:42,500 --> 00:24:45,730
the more cars on the road,
the more difficult it is

434
00:24:45,730 --> 00:24:47,570
to evacuate the town.

435
00:24:47,570 --> 00:24:51,670
So we didn't have a plan to
evacuate the entire town at once

436
00:24:51,670 --> 00:24:54,430
mostly because it wouldn't work.

437
00:24:54,430 --> 00:24:56,430
Our plan became, I think,
probably one of

438
00:24:56,430 --> 00:24:59,400
the most elaborate plans
in the state.

439
00:24:59,400 --> 00:25:02,870
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: In a review after
the 2008 fire,

440
00:25:02,870 --> 00:25:05,870
a Butte county grand jury
warned that the town's roads

441
00:25:05,870 --> 00:25:08,470
had "serious capacity
limitations"

442
00:25:08,470 --> 00:25:10,570
and made a number of
recommendations,

443
00:25:10,570 --> 00:25:14,230
including widening
the evacuation routes.

444
00:25:14,230 --> 00:25:16,370
The county's governing board
implemented

445
00:25:16,370 --> 00:25:19,030
some of the recommendations,
but there was no funding

446
00:25:19,030 --> 00:25:21,270
to widen all of the roads.

447
00:25:21,270 --> 00:25:22,700
♪ ♪

448
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:25,900
&gt;&gt; One of my personal responses
to the grand jury was,

449
00:25:25,900 --> 00:25:29,830
if you gave us
$10 or $15 million,

450
00:25:29,830 --> 00:25:33,900
maybe $20 million, to build
new roads off the ridge,

451
00:25:33,900 --> 00:25:36,970
um, maybe we could develop
a plan

452
00:25:36,970 --> 00:25:38,570
that would get people off
the ridge,

453
00:25:38,570 --> 00:25:41,230
you know, everyone off the
ridge at one time.

454
00:25:41,230 --> 00:25:42,830
Roads cost a lot of money.

455
00:25:42,830 --> 00:25:45,700
These roads would be roads that,
on a average day,

456
00:25:45,700 --> 00:25:47,930
they're built for traffic that
doesn't exist.

457
00:25:47,930 --> 00:25:49,670
And then you say we're going
to build four lanes

458
00:25:49,670 --> 00:25:55,200
that aren't gonna be used except
once in a, in a half-century?

459
00:25:55,200 --> 00:26:01,070
Yeah, that, that's gonna be
a pretty hard ask to make.

460
00:26:01,070 --> 00:26:03,170
(people talking on radio)

461
00:26:03,170 --> 00:26:06,800
&gt;&gt; ...gonna open up both lanes
and get everybody out.

462
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:09,400
(people talking on radio)

463
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:12,300
&gt;&gt; Flames, get people moving,
now!

464
00:26:12,300 --> 00:26:15,700
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: There were now over
350 firefighters in Paradise.

465
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:19,700
But with burning embers causing
new fires all across the town,

466
00:26:19,700 --> 00:26:22,570
there was no clear front line
for them to fight.

467
00:26:22,570 --> 00:26:25,200
(people talking on radio)

468
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:26,470
&gt;&gt; We conceded...

469
00:26:26,470 --> 00:26:28,370
I can tell you, it was 9:23 in
the morning,

470
00:26:28,370 --> 00:26:32,800
we conceded that maintaining
the evacuation routes

471
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:35,500
and civilian rescue was
our only objective that day,

472
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:39,570
and there was no orders given
that contradicted that.

473
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:42,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR:
Although the entire town

474
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:44,630
was under an evacuation order,

475
00:26:44,630 --> 00:26:47,500
thousands of residents
were still at home.

476
00:26:47,500 --> 00:26:49,800
(sirens blaring)

477
00:26:54,430 --> 00:26:56,570
&gt;&gt; My mom had me at 41.

478
00:26:56,570 --> 00:26:59,100
For many years, we were like
best friends.

479
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:01,700
We would rent out Redbox movies
from Safeway,

480
00:27:01,700 --> 00:27:03,730
which was right next door,
and hang out.

481
00:27:03,730 --> 00:27:05,430
And I could tell her anything.

482
00:27:05,430 --> 00:27:08,070
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: 25-year-old
Christina Taft and her mother,

483
00:27:08,070 --> 00:27:11,800
Victoria,
lived in central Paradise.

484
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:14,230
&gt;&gt; I wasn't thinking it
was that serious at first,

485
00:27:14,230 --> 00:27:17,770
and then in the shower,
I started to smell smoke.

486
00:27:17,770 --> 00:27:18,970
I was definitely panicked.

487
00:27:18,970 --> 00:27:21,700
I thought it could all,
like, burn.

488
00:27:21,700 --> 00:27:23,630
And I told that to my mom,
and she just...

489
00:27:23,630 --> 00:27:26,000
she didn't want to listen
to that negativity.

490
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:27,630
We weren't really, like,
arguing,

491
00:27:27,630 --> 00:27:29,500
it was just kind of like
I was saying stuff

492
00:27:29,500 --> 00:27:32,200
and then packing up everything
I could into the car.

493
00:27:32,200 --> 00:27:35,470
Like, it was completely filled
in the trunk and the back seat,

494
00:27:35,470 --> 00:27:40,030
and just with the front seat,
you know, for my mom.

495
00:27:40,030 --> 00:27:42,100
It went on for a hour, kind of.

496
00:27:42,100 --> 00:27:44,170
She was just not really packing,

497
00:27:44,170 --> 00:27:45,770
she didn't get out of her
pajamas,

498
00:27:45,770 --> 00:27:47,070
and then she started calling
other people

499
00:27:47,070 --> 00:27:49,530
to find out what was happening.

500
00:27:49,530 --> 00:27:51,600
Looking outside, it started
getting, you know,

501
00:27:51,600 --> 00:27:55,070
traffic and darker.

502
00:27:55,070 --> 00:27:57,230
I, you know, I just didn't know
what to do.

503
00:27:57,230 --> 00:28:00,730
Like, it was either I leave
or stay and risk my own life,

504
00:28:00,730 --> 00:28:02,330
and I had a life to live.

505
00:28:02,330 --> 00:28:04,870
Like, I told her that, like,
"I have a life to live."

506
00:28:04,870 --> 00:28:08,830
And she was just kind of, like,
talking to people on the phone,

507
00:28:08,830 --> 00:28:12,770
and they weren't telling her,
"Leave."

508
00:28:12,770 --> 00:28:14,800
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Christina joined
the thousands of others

509
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:17,030
evacuating the town.

510
00:28:17,030 --> 00:28:20,300
Her mom refused
to come with her.

511
00:28:20,300 --> 00:28:23,200
&gt;&gt; It was very slow leaving, but
it was all burnt,

512
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:25,670
like, all the way down.

513
00:28:25,670 --> 00:28:28,230
People were stopping,
getting people in their cars,

514
00:28:28,230 --> 00:28:30,100
and I was stuck,
so I couldn't go back,

515
00:28:30,100 --> 00:28:32,200
even though it wasn't
very far away.

516
00:28:32,200 --> 00:28:35,400
It just was horrible,
because I kept calling my mom,

517
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:37,670
and it just didn't work.

518
00:28:37,670 --> 00:28:40,030
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Christina and her
mother had not received

519
00:28:40,030 --> 00:28:42,500
an official evacuation order.

520
00:28:42,500 --> 00:28:44,830
The county sheriff's office was
using a new alert system

521
00:28:44,830 --> 00:28:48,300
called Code Red.

522
00:28:48,300 --> 00:28:50,770
It had an option to send out a
mass alert

523
00:28:50,770 --> 00:28:56,300
to every Paradise resident, but
that morning they didn't use it.

524
00:28:58,570 --> 00:29:01,800
&gt;&gt; This was an extraordinarily
chaotic situation.

525
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:05,600
There was difficulty in terms
of structuring

526
00:29:05,600 --> 00:29:11,270
the, um, the area that
we wanted to target.

527
00:29:11,270 --> 00:29:13,500
We had one person who was
working

528
00:29:13,500 --> 00:29:16,200
to try to get that message out.

529
00:29:16,200 --> 00:29:17,600
I can assure you from the
standpoint

530
00:29:17,600 --> 00:29:21,100
of the sheriff's office, nobody
was waiting around,

531
00:29:21,100 --> 00:29:25,130
uh, uh... to notify people.

532
00:29:25,130 --> 00:29:26,330
It wasn't as though this...

533
00:29:26,330 --> 00:29:29,400
any delay was calculated or
intentional.

534
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:32,100
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: They did send out
alerts

535
00:29:32,100 --> 00:29:35,870
using another feature that
informed residents zone by zone,

536
00:29:35,870 --> 00:29:39,770
but only those
who had signed up.

537
00:29:39,770 --> 00:29:42,670
&gt;&gt; We knew that sign-ups were
not where they needed to be.

538
00:29:42,670 --> 00:29:44,730
But we believed that that was
the future,

539
00:29:44,730 --> 00:29:47,370
and our big campaign for 2019

540
00:29:47,370 --> 00:29:50,770
was to really increase the
number of people signed up

541
00:29:50,770 --> 00:29:52,230
for Code Red.

542
00:29:52,230 --> 00:29:54,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: More than half of
residents had not signed up,

543
00:29:54,570 --> 00:29:57,630
including Christina
and her mother.

544
00:29:57,630 --> 00:30:01,830
And many of those who had still
didn't get a notification.

545
00:30:01,830 --> 00:30:03,970
&gt;&gt; Cell phone towers went down--

546
00:30:03,970 --> 00:30:06,100
the networks were so clogged
that we couldn't get through it.

547
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:10,270
It was an event that literally
outpaced all of our resources

548
00:30:10,270 --> 00:30:14,270
almost immediately, literally
outpaced all of the planning

549
00:30:14,270 --> 00:30:17,630
that had been done
prior to this.

550
00:30:17,630 --> 00:30:20,570
And ultimately,
people have to be responsible

551
00:30:20,570 --> 00:30:23,300
for their own safety.

552
00:30:23,300 --> 00:30:30,230
The best person to craft an
evacuation plan for you is you.

553
00:30:30,230 --> 00:30:33,300
♪ ♪

554
00:30:38,030 --> 00:30:40,670
&gt;&gt; This is me trying to
evacuate.

555
00:30:40,670 --> 00:30:44,330
Pentz Road is on fire,
everything is burnt.

556
00:30:46,600 --> 00:30:48,730
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: After evacuating
the hospital,

557
00:30:48,730 --> 00:30:52,330
nurse Nichole Jolly
was driving south.

558
00:30:52,330 --> 00:30:56,870
She turned off Pentz Road onto
a side street, Pearson Road.

559
00:30:56,870 --> 00:31:02,370
Ahead of her, cars were already
on fire and had been abandoned.

560
00:31:02,370 --> 00:31:05,800
&gt;&gt; I'm getting down into this
ravine, and I kind of look,

561
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:08,370
going, "Oh, this,
this isn't good,"

562
00:31:08,370 --> 00:31:11,800
because this fire is blowing
so fast.

563
00:31:11,800 --> 00:31:14,300
&gt;&gt; The road's completely
engulfed in flames,

564
00:31:14,300 --> 00:31:19,370
and we're stuck
in the middle of it.

565
00:31:19,370 --> 00:31:22,770
That tree could come on me at
any moment.

566
00:31:22,770 --> 00:31:24,570
This is ridiculous,
and I'm stuck behind

567
00:31:24,570 --> 00:31:27,630
these stupid (bleep).

568
00:31:27,630 --> 00:31:29,670
&gt;&gt; And I'm on my, on the phone
to my husband,

569
00:31:29,670 --> 00:31:31,700
and I'm screaming for him.

570
00:31:31,700 --> 00:31:34,670
I'm, like, "Nick, you got to get
to me, you have to hurry,

571
00:31:34,670 --> 00:31:36,830
because I'm not going
to make it."

572
00:31:36,830 --> 00:31:40,870
And he said, "I'm trying,
I'm going to get to you."

573
00:31:40,870 --> 00:31:47,330
And I'm, like, "I'm going to
die, and I'm, I'm so sorry."

574
00:31:47,330 --> 00:31:49,170
And... and my car is starting to
fill up with smoke

575
00:31:49,170 --> 00:31:50,470
at that point.

576
00:31:50,470 --> 00:31:51,730
And I told my husband,
I'm, like,

577
00:31:51,730 --> 00:31:52,830
"The car's filling up
with smoke,

578
00:31:52,830 --> 00:31:54,100
I have to get out of the car."

579
00:31:54,100 --> 00:31:56,230
And he's, like,
"Get out and run."

580
00:31:56,230 --> 00:31:59,800
And I'm, like, "I can't get out
and run, you don't understand,

581
00:31:59,800 --> 00:32:04,100
there's fire everywhere,
and I can't run through fire."

582
00:32:04,100 --> 00:32:06,730
And he said, "You're going
to have to."

583
00:32:08,600 --> 00:32:14,530
♪ ♪

584
00:32:14,530 --> 00:32:18,030
&gt;&gt; The town of Paradise,
almost more than any other town

585
00:32:18,030 --> 00:32:22,370
that I've heard of, had really
thought about the issue of fire

586
00:32:22,370 --> 00:32:24,870
and evacuation,
and they had a plan.

587
00:32:24,870 --> 00:32:28,570
And the plan was completely
overwhelmed by circumstances.

588
00:32:28,570 --> 00:32:34,230
I think those circumstances
were not unprecedented.

589
00:32:34,230 --> 00:32:37,870
We have had a number of fires
over the last several years

590
00:32:37,870 --> 00:32:42,670
prior to the Camp Fire that had
some of the characteristics,

591
00:32:42,670 --> 00:32:45,530
in particular,
the rate of spread

592
00:32:45,530 --> 00:32:47,900
and the total ineffectiveness

593
00:32:47,900 --> 00:32:52,530
of any kind
of suppression effort.

594
00:32:52,530 --> 00:32:54,870
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Climate change has
contributed to making fires

595
00:32:54,870 --> 00:32:58,630
bigger and more frequent.

596
00:32:58,630 --> 00:33:01,730
Ten of the 20 most destructive
fires in California

597
00:33:01,730 --> 00:33:04,530
have happened
in the last four years.

598
00:33:04,530 --> 00:33:06,230
&gt;&gt; Fires are different today.

599
00:33:06,230 --> 00:33:07,600
You need to plan differently.

600
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:08,570
You have communities that are
saying,

601
00:33:08,570 --> 00:33:10,530
"We have our evacuation plan."

602
00:33:10,530 --> 00:33:13,130
But if the plan involves driving
down a road

603
00:33:13,130 --> 00:33:16,130
like the one in Paradise,
that was essentially blocked

604
00:33:16,130 --> 00:33:19,700
by the fire,
that's not a very good plan.

605
00:33:19,700 --> 00:33:23,000
If the road is narrow
and will become gridlocked,

606
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:26,230
not a very good plan.

607
00:33:26,230 --> 00:33:30,930
&gt;&gt; So, in about 2015,
we developed this binder.

608
00:33:30,930 --> 00:33:32,900
And we carry this binder
in our vehicles.

609
00:33:32,900 --> 00:33:34,830
And this binder includes
evacuation plans

610
00:33:34,830 --> 00:33:38,400
and traffic plans, um...
evacuation plans

611
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:41,670
for every community, foothill
community in Butte County.

612
00:33:41,670 --> 00:33:45,730
&gt;&gt; Why, given that there have
been very fast-moving fires

613
00:33:45,730 --> 00:33:49,000
before, was it not part
of the planning

614
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,170
that it might be a possibility
to have a fire

615
00:33:51,170 --> 00:33:53,730
of this speed and intensity?

616
00:33:53,730 --> 00:33:55,730
&gt;&gt; I don't think we've ever seen
that before.

617
00:33:55,730 --> 00:33:57,870
So I don't think that it was
something that was,

618
00:33:57,870 --> 00:33:59,370
that was ever envisioned.

619
00:33:59,370 --> 00:34:02,530
As far as modeling, we did plan
for a rapidly developing fire.

620
00:34:02,530 --> 00:34:05,830
We just did not plan for... we
just did not anticipate a fire

621
00:34:05,830 --> 00:34:08,170
that went seven-and-a-half miles
in an hour and a half.

622
00:34:08,170 --> 00:34:10,330
I don't think anybody envisioned
that happening.

623
00:34:10,330 --> 00:34:13,930
&gt;&gt; Do you think you should have
envisioned that happening?

624
00:34:13,930 --> 00:34:17,130
&gt;&gt; I, I'm not going
to answer that question.

625
00:34:17,130 --> 00:34:18,830
♪ ♪

626
00:34:18,830 --> 00:34:20,870
&gt;&gt; We've got four trapped
in the basement.

627
00:34:20,870 --> 00:34:23,770
Four people trapped
in the basement.

628
00:34:23,770 --> 00:34:26,730
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: An hour and a half
after fire hit Paradise,

629
00:34:26,730 --> 00:34:28,900
thousands were trying to leave,

630
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:33,070
but many others were trapped
in their homes.

631
00:34:33,070 --> 00:34:36,530
18 miles from the fire,
Cal Fire's emergency center

632
00:34:36,530 --> 00:34:40,100
was receiving 911 calls.

633
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:43,030
&gt;&gt; The phones rang and rang and
rang, and they didn't stop.

634
00:34:50,770 --> 00:34:54,430
&gt;&gt; It was loud, it was, it was
noisy, it was constant.

635
00:35:06,470 --> 00:35:09,500
&gt;&gt; I answered the phone,
and I heard a lady--

636
00:35:09,500 --> 00:35:11,230
actually, I heard three ladies.

637
00:35:18,730 --> 00:35:21,030
&gt;&gt; They were coughing, choking.

638
00:35:21,030 --> 00:35:22,400
She had a hard time
even telling me

639
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:23,730
exactly where they were.

640
00:35:30,830 --> 00:35:32,930
&gt;&gt; They were in a room with,
she told me,

641
00:35:32,930 --> 00:35:35,330
"No windows,
I, I can't get out."

642
00:35:35,330 --> 00:35:38,100
And I couldn't...
I couldn't leave her.

643
00:35:49,770 --> 00:35:52,300
&gt;&gt; It started getting
real staticky.

644
00:35:52,300 --> 00:35:54,370
And I had no response.

645
00:35:54,370 --> 00:35:56,670
I was talking to myself.

646
00:35:56,670 --> 00:36:02,470
And after nine minutes and, and
something, the phone went dead.

647
00:36:02,470 --> 00:36:04,630
I just couldn't help her.

648
00:36:04,630 --> 00:36:08,870
And I just had to hit
the next...

649
00:36:08,870 --> 00:36:12,430
answer the 911,
and start all over.

650
00:36:15,800 --> 00:36:17,230
(people talking in background)

651
00:36:17,230 --> 00:36:21,530
♪ ♪

652
00:36:30,200 --> 00:36:32,530
&gt;&gt; Here?

653
00:36:32,530 --> 00:36:33,470
&gt;&gt; I gotta go.

654
00:36:41,200 --> 00:36:43,930
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: By midmorning,
firefighters were trying

655
00:36:43,930 --> 00:36:47,470
to make it down the road where
Nichole Jolly was stranded.

656
00:36:47,470 --> 00:36:49,030
The temperature at the center of
the fire

657
00:36:49,030 --> 00:36:51,370
was now around 1,800 degrees.

658
00:37:04,430 --> 00:37:08,230
&gt;&gt; I'm running up this hill,
and it's a pretty steep hill,

659
00:37:08,230 --> 00:37:10,370
and I couldn't see anything.

660
00:37:10,370 --> 00:37:13,430
And I'm putting my hand over my
eyes,

661
00:37:13,430 --> 00:37:19,270
and the flames are just hitting
the side of me.

662
00:37:19,270 --> 00:37:22,500
I just was thinking, "Please let
there be a vehicle or something

663
00:37:22,500 --> 00:37:25,970
that I can jump into," 'cause
I was so hot at that point.

664
00:37:25,970 --> 00:37:29,400
And I ended up touching
the back of a fire engine,

665
00:37:29,400 --> 00:37:34,130
and I'm, like, "Oh, yay,
a fire engine."

666
00:37:34,130 --> 00:37:39,770
I sat in the center, and we were
stuck, we were stopped.

667
00:37:39,770 --> 00:37:41,430
And I'm, like,
"Why aren't we moving?"

668
00:37:41,430 --> 00:37:45,100
And he's, like, "Well, there's
cars on fire all around us."

669
00:37:45,100 --> 00:37:46,400
Look, we're in a fire engine,

670
00:37:46,400 --> 00:37:48,630
this is what this thing is built
for, you know.

671
00:37:48,630 --> 00:37:52,070
It's, this thing's meant to go
through fire.

672
00:37:52,070 --> 00:37:56,230
No, those things are not meant
to go through fire.

673
00:37:56,230 --> 00:38:00,400
&gt;&gt; I could start hearing
a distress call for air support.

674
00:38:00,400 --> 00:38:03,230
And you could hear the urgency
in their voices on the radio.

675
00:38:03,230 --> 00:38:06,270
I remember it being pitch-black
outside and zero visibility

676
00:38:06,270 --> 00:38:08,700
and knowing that that was
impossible.

677
00:38:08,700 --> 00:38:10,700
I answered him back,
inappropriately,

678
00:38:10,700 --> 00:38:12,600
uh, using his first name.

679
00:38:12,600 --> 00:38:14,570
I said, "John, where are you?"

680
00:38:16,730 --> 00:38:18,730
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Determined to get
to his colleagues,

681
00:38:18,730 --> 00:38:22,930
Joe Kennedy drove a bulldozer
through the flames.

682
00:38:22,930 --> 00:38:25,730
&gt;&gt; We're hearing this noise
coming up behind us.

683
00:38:25,730 --> 00:38:27,130
It was really loud.

684
00:38:27,130 --> 00:38:29,900
It was this clinking chains.

685
00:38:29,900 --> 00:38:32,870
You could hear, it was, like,
thunk-thunk-thunk-thunk-thunk.

686
00:38:32,870 --> 00:38:34,930
&gt;&gt; I started taking fully
involved vehicles

687
00:38:34,930 --> 00:38:37,270
and moving them away
the best I could.

688
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:40,900
&gt;&gt; And he's flipping them over,
and it's just a miracle.

689
00:38:40,900 --> 00:38:44,370
And he cleared this way for us.

690
00:38:44,370 --> 00:38:47,330
&gt;&gt; What happened on Pearson Road
we don't train for.

691
00:38:47,330 --> 00:38:49,970
They don't teach us how to move
fully involved cars.

692
00:38:49,970 --> 00:38:52,230
They teach us how to avoid that.

693
00:38:52,230 --> 00:38:54,800
There were several times where
it crossed my mind

694
00:38:54,800 --> 00:38:57,070
that this was a very bad idea.

695
00:38:57,070 --> 00:38:59,100
But if people were counting on
me to keep going

696
00:38:59,100 --> 00:39:02,300
and, uh, not stop.

697
00:39:02,300 --> 00:39:04,270
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Joe Kennedy managed
to clear the road

698
00:39:04,270 --> 00:39:08,000
so Nichole and the firefighters
could get to safety.

699
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:11,300
He continued working
for another 24 hours.

700
00:39:11,300 --> 00:39:14,200
&gt;&gt; He kept saving people
on that road.

701
00:39:14,200 --> 00:39:19,030
No A/C, no fire blankets,
just glass windows

702
00:39:19,030 --> 00:39:22,200
in the middle of this inferno.

703
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:24,930
♪ ♪

704
00:39:24,930 --> 00:39:28,870
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: The fire had now
burned around 20,000 acres

705
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:31,030
and was visible from space.

706
00:39:31,030 --> 00:39:35,130
♪ ♪

707
00:39:35,130 --> 00:39:38,830
&gt;&gt; 21-54...

708
00:39:38,830 --> 00:39:43,570
(people talking on radio)

709
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:49,500
&gt;&gt; Our air-tac officer
gave a report,

710
00:39:49,500 --> 00:39:53,930
where the fire was
and how much was being impacted.

711
00:39:53,930 --> 00:39:55,670
He basically said,
"The fire's progressed

712
00:39:55,670 --> 00:39:59,570
all the way through town."

713
00:39:59,570 --> 00:40:02,770
And these reports of civilians
trapped and rescues,

714
00:40:02,770 --> 00:40:04,770
and, you know,
we'd already had reports

715
00:40:04,770 --> 00:40:06,230
of a lot of fatalities.

716
00:40:06,230 --> 00:40:07,630
&gt;&gt; 70 Charlie...

717
00:40:07,630 --> 00:40:10,170
&gt;&gt; And by noon, we had conceded

718
00:40:10,170 --> 00:40:12,830
that the town had basically
burned down.

719
00:40:12,830 --> 00:40:17,300
(people talking on radio)

720
00:40:17,300 --> 00:40:18,570
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: It took only
four hours for Paradise

721
00:40:18,570 --> 00:40:22,130
to be destroyed.

722
00:40:22,130 --> 00:40:25,630
By the end of the day, 50,000
people had managed to escape,

723
00:40:25,630 --> 00:40:28,630
scattering to neighboring towns.

724
00:40:28,630 --> 00:40:30,630
&gt;&gt; There was literally a point
on the road

725
00:40:30,630 --> 00:40:34,570
where it went from hell to,
there was a sky again

726
00:40:34,570 --> 00:40:36,130
and there was air to breathe.

727
00:40:36,130 --> 00:40:37,530
And it was this type of feeling

728
00:40:37,530 --> 00:40:39,670
that changes your whole
entire life.

729
00:40:39,670 --> 00:40:43,930
"I just got this chance to be
able to live again."

730
00:40:43,930 --> 00:40:45,700
&gt;&gt; My mom took us back
to the house

731
00:40:45,700 --> 00:40:47,830
that my kids were staying at.

732
00:40:47,830 --> 00:40:51,270
And I see my husband
just pacing in the driveway,

733
00:40:51,270 --> 00:40:53,630
and he's just pacing and pacing
and pacing.

734
00:40:53,630 --> 00:40:55,700
And I'm, like, "Mom, you need to
go, you need to get down there,

735
00:40:55,700 --> 00:40:57,300
I see Nick."

736
00:40:57,300 --> 00:40:59,000
And she's, like, "Nichole,
we're in a residential area--

737
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:00,500
I can't, I can't drive fast."

738
00:41:00,500 --> 00:41:02,730
And I'm, like,
"Then you need to let me out."

739
00:41:02,730 --> 00:41:06,630
And I got out of the car, and I
ran faster than she was driving,

740
00:41:06,630 --> 00:41:09,070
and I just grabbed
onto my husband.

741
00:41:09,070 --> 00:41:10,770
And I'll never forget what he
said.

742
00:41:10,770 --> 00:41:13,230
He said, "I thought
I almost lost you."

743
00:41:13,230 --> 00:41:15,300
And I'm, like, "I know."

744
00:41:15,300 --> 00:41:21,800
♪ ♪

745
00:41:28,430 --> 00:41:29,970
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: A week after the
fire started,

746
00:41:29,970 --> 00:41:34,170
more than 5,000 firefighters
were tackling the blaze,

747
00:41:34,170 --> 00:41:37,030
from the ground and the sky.

748
00:41:37,030 --> 00:41:42,530
♪ ♪

749
00:41:42,530 --> 00:41:44,200
&gt;&gt; There was nothing standing,

750
00:41:44,200 --> 00:41:46,470
and there were still homes
burning.

751
00:41:46,470 --> 00:41:48,970
You know, power lines were down,
cars were burned,

752
00:41:48,970 --> 00:41:50,630
they were still burning.

753
00:41:50,630 --> 00:41:52,270
It looked like a war zone,

754
00:41:52,270 --> 00:41:58,070
it looked like bombs had been
dropped on the town.

755
00:41:58,070 --> 00:42:00,970
&gt;&gt; It was heartbreaking to me.

756
00:42:00,970 --> 00:42:02,670
I grew up in that town,

757
00:42:02,670 --> 00:42:04,830
um, I graduated from high school
in that town.

758
00:42:04,830 --> 00:42:06,500
I was the fire chief
in that town

759
00:42:06,500 --> 00:42:09,570
and honored to be
the fire chief in that town,

760
00:42:09,570 --> 00:42:11,600
and it was heartbreaking to see.

761
00:42:11,600 --> 00:42:15,230
♪ ♪

762
00:42:21,130 --> 00:42:24,330
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Paradise burned
for over two weeks.

763
00:42:24,330 --> 00:42:30,100
Finally, the first winter rains
came and put the fire out.

764
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:35,070
It had burned 153,000 acres,
an area the size of Chicago.

765
00:42:38,130 --> 00:42:43,930
♪ ♪

766
00:42:43,930 --> 00:42:48,600
It was the most destructive fire
California had ever seen.

767
00:42:48,600 --> 00:42:52,000
Around 30,000 people
lost their homes.

768
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:54,270
It took many weeks to identify
those who died.

769
00:42:58,030 --> 00:43:03,130
&gt;&gt; It was actually Thanksgiving
Day when they confirmed it.

770
00:43:03,130 --> 00:43:05,200
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Christina Taft had
not heard from her mother

771
00:43:05,200 --> 00:43:07,630
since the morning of the fire.

772
00:43:07,630 --> 00:43:11,030
&gt;&gt; She was found on the property
in the living room.

773
00:43:11,030 --> 00:43:13,430
She was still inside,
she wasn't able to get out.

774
00:43:13,430 --> 00:43:16,400
And probably, like,
it was right by the window,

775
00:43:16,400 --> 00:43:18,230
so that was really horrible
imagining

776
00:43:18,230 --> 00:43:22,030
that she didn't probably know
what to do or something.

777
00:43:25,200 --> 00:43:26,530
I don't think
she really realized

778
00:43:26,530 --> 00:43:29,600
it was as bad as it was.

779
00:43:29,600 --> 00:43:31,870
I blamed myself,
I blamed authority,

780
00:43:31,870 --> 00:43:33,700
I blamed the other people,

781
00:43:33,700 --> 00:43:38,000
I blamed a lot of things,
and, um...

782
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:41,030
I'm not really angry at her.

783
00:43:41,030 --> 00:43:42,830
People, I think they expect if
there's an emergency,

784
00:43:42,830 --> 00:43:44,570
they'll get notified.

785
00:43:44,570 --> 00:43:46,270
I think if we did have a order,

786
00:43:46,270 --> 00:43:49,000
it would have made a difference
to my mom.

787
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:52,230
♪ ♪

788
00:43:52,230 --> 00:43:56,430
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: 85 people perished
in the Camp Fire.

789
00:43:56,430 --> 00:44:00,830
The majority were over
65 years old.

790
00:44:00,830 --> 00:44:03,030
Some were trapped in their cars,

791
00:44:03,030 --> 00:44:07,030
others were still
in their homes.

792
00:44:07,030 --> 00:44:11,200
&gt;&gt; It breaks my heart that they
got a false sense of security.

793
00:44:11,200 --> 00:44:15,670
It breaks my heart that
I and anybody else

794
00:44:15,670 --> 00:44:17,400
that was answering the phone
that day

795
00:44:17,400 --> 00:44:20,300
was not able to give them more
information,

796
00:44:20,300 --> 00:44:25,100
better information,
faster information.

797
00:44:25,100 --> 00:44:29,470
It kind of snowballs on you.

798
00:44:29,470 --> 00:44:33,370
♪ ♪

799
00:44:39,230 --> 00:44:43,000
&gt;&gt; Could you have got evacuation
orders out to communities

800
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:47,100
that were likely to be hit
before they were hit?

801
00:44:47,100 --> 00:44:53,500
&gt;&gt; I mean, we can always Monday,
you know, Monday quarterback it.

802
00:44:53,500 --> 00:44:55,430
I know what you're saying, but,
no, I mean, maybe,

803
00:44:55,430 --> 00:44:58,000
maybe five minutes earlier.

804
00:44:58,000 --> 00:45:02,970
But the issue wasn't how fast
we notified the public,

805
00:45:02,970 --> 00:45:06,800
it was how fast we could get
them off the hill.

806
00:45:06,800 --> 00:45:10,170
The transportation system would
only hold so many vehicles,

807
00:45:10,170 --> 00:45:12,900
and we were trying to put more
vehicles on the road

808
00:45:12,900 --> 00:45:15,270
than it could hold.

809
00:45:15,270 --> 00:45:20,030
♪ ♪

810
00:45:20,030 --> 00:45:22,530
&gt;&gt; I have no doubt in my mind
that if we,

811
00:45:22,530 --> 00:45:25,730
as public safety agencies,
had not done what we did,

812
00:45:25,730 --> 00:45:27,230
the conditions would have been
much worse

813
00:45:27,230 --> 00:45:29,070
and there would have been more
loss of life.

814
00:45:29,070 --> 00:45:30,430
It was bad.

815
00:45:30,430 --> 00:45:32,400
But this fire affected tens of
thousands of people

816
00:45:32,400 --> 00:45:34,230
in a matter of a few hours.

817
00:45:34,230 --> 00:45:35,700
The plan was implemented.

818
00:45:35,700 --> 00:45:39,900
I, I'm very confident in saying
it was, it was successful.

819
00:45:39,900 --> 00:45:41,100
Was it flawless?

820
00:45:41,100 --> 00:45:42,670
Absolutely not.

821
00:45:42,670 --> 00:45:48,400
♪ ♪

822
00:45:48,400 --> 00:45:49,830
&gt;&gt; We never gave up hope.

823
00:45:49,830 --> 00:45:53,730
You know, we kept looking,
and he can't read or write,

824
00:45:53,730 --> 00:45:57,870
so we thought maybe he couldn't
get in contact with us.

825
00:45:57,870 --> 00:45:59,470
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Jordan Huff was
waiting for news

826
00:45:59,470 --> 00:46:04,030
about her grandfather TK,
who had been up in Concow.

827
00:46:04,030 --> 00:46:06,170
&gt;&gt; It was two weeks later,
my mom called me,

828
00:46:06,170 --> 00:46:09,970
and she was all like,
"Jordan..."

829
00:46:09,970 --> 00:46:11,630
I knew what the phone call was,

830
00:46:11,630 --> 00:46:15,030
because, like, my mom doesn't
call to talk.

831
00:46:15,030 --> 00:46:18,370
She told me they found Pop's
body, and I was like, "Yeah?"

832
00:46:18,370 --> 00:46:21,630
And then like, "Yeah, they found
the body in the home."

833
00:46:21,630 --> 00:46:24,070
And I was like, "Oh,"
and, you know, I just cried.

834
00:46:24,070 --> 00:46:26,670
I didn't know what to say,
and she asked me if I'm okay,

835
00:46:26,670 --> 00:46:32,000
and I just hung up the phone,
because you're not okay.

836
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:36,030
We went out there on December 4,
me and my dad only.

837
00:46:36,030 --> 00:46:39,430
Literally, everything is gone
except, you know,

838
00:46:39,430 --> 00:46:43,070
you go out to the back fence,
and you see a wheelchair.

839
00:46:43,070 --> 00:46:46,500
You see his watering hose burnt
to a crisp all the way,

840
00:46:46,500 --> 00:46:48,700
dragged all the way right
next to the wheelchair

841
00:46:48,700 --> 00:46:51,370
and a bucket of water.

842
00:46:51,370 --> 00:46:55,570
Your mind, like,
wants to make a image.

843
00:46:55,570 --> 00:46:58,130
But you don't really want
to make an image,

844
00:46:58,130 --> 00:47:00,670
but it does it anyways.

845
00:47:00,670 --> 00:47:05,400
And... and man, is it crazy to
have a image like that

846
00:47:05,400 --> 00:47:08,730
in your head.

847
00:47:08,730 --> 00:47:14,670
He was insanely tough and smart,
and he was a gentle giant.

848
00:47:18,500 --> 00:47:23,300
♪ ♪

849
00:47:23,300 --> 00:47:24,770
&gt;&gt; Just going around the
community,

850
00:47:24,770 --> 00:47:28,200
and you see someone that you
haven't seen for a while.

851
00:47:28,200 --> 00:47:30,270
"Where were you?
What happened to you?

852
00:47:30,270 --> 00:47:31,770
What happened to your family?"

853
00:47:31,770 --> 00:47:34,070
It's our local 9/11.

854
00:47:34,070 --> 00:47:37,570
This is a day that we will
always remember.

855
00:47:37,570 --> 00:47:40,470
November 8 will always be
a date that'll just...

856
00:47:40,470 --> 00:47:44,870
seared in the collective
consciousness of our community.

857
00:47:46,170 --> 00:47:47,400
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Six months after
the fire,

858
00:47:47,400 --> 00:47:49,370
the Butte County District
Attorney launched

859
00:47:49,370 --> 00:47:52,270
an investigation into whether to
bring criminal charges

860
00:47:52,270 --> 00:47:58,070
against PG&amp;E, the company whose
power line had started the fire.

861
00:47:58,070 --> 00:48:04,600
&gt;&gt; Is what PG&amp;E did-- or did not
do-- grossly negligent?

862
00:48:04,600 --> 00:48:11,030
Something that is beyond,
well beyond ordinary negligence?

863
00:48:11,030 --> 00:48:13,230
One of the charges
that we're looking at

864
00:48:13,230 --> 00:48:18,430
under California Penal Code
Section 452 is reckless arson.

865
00:48:18,430 --> 00:48:22,130
"To prove the defendant
is guilty of this crime,

866
00:48:22,130 --> 00:48:25,900
the people must prove beyond a
reasonable doubt that,

867
00:48:25,900 --> 00:48:32,670
one, the defendant burned or
caused to be burned property,

868
00:48:32,670 --> 00:48:36,000
uh, or forest land"--
pretty simple.

869
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:37,500
We've got that.

870
00:48:37,500 --> 00:48:40,970
That the fired burned
an inhabited structure

871
00:48:40,970 --> 00:48:44,370
or the fire caused great bodily
injury to another person.

872
00:48:44,370 --> 00:48:48,600
Okay, we've got structures--
nearly 14,000-- 85 people.

873
00:48:48,600 --> 00:48:50,030
Got that element.

874
00:48:50,030 --> 00:48:53,400
The element that is the-the last
element, it says,

875
00:48:53,400 --> 00:48:57,970
"And the defendant did so
recklessly."

876
00:48:57,970 --> 00:49:01,800
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: PG&amp;E has a long
history of safety violations

877
00:49:01,800 --> 00:49:05,670
and a criminal conviction for
a gas explosion in 2010.

878
00:49:05,670 --> 00:49:08,200
Its equipment has been linked to
many destructive fires

879
00:49:08,200 --> 00:49:11,100
in California in recent years.

880
00:49:11,100 --> 00:49:13,630
&gt;&gt; This is a company that,
it was fined hundreds of times

881
00:49:13,630 --> 00:49:17,670
and faced more than two, almost
$3 billion worth of fines.

882
00:49:17,670 --> 00:49:20,070
You know, if PG&amp;E was an
individual

883
00:49:20,070 --> 00:49:23,670
and not a corporation, I think
by now they would be in prison.

884
00:49:23,670 --> 00:49:25,530
There's just been repeat
offenders,

885
00:49:25,530 --> 00:49:28,300
they've been on probation,
they've violated the probation.

886
00:49:28,300 --> 00:49:30,270
The problem is you can't take
a corporation

887
00:49:30,270 --> 00:49:32,800
and put it into prison.

888
00:49:32,800 --> 00:49:34,300
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: In the months after
the fire,

889
00:49:34,300 --> 00:49:36,230
reporters at "The Wall Street
Journal" discovered

890
00:49:36,230 --> 00:49:40,370
that PG&amp;E had been warned its
transmission towers were aging

891
00:49:40,370 --> 00:49:42,770
and that components might fail.

892
00:49:42,770 --> 00:49:45,770
&gt;&gt; In 2010, they had an outside
contractor come in

893
00:49:45,770 --> 00:49:46,930
and they looked at this
and said,

894
00:49:46,930 --> 00:49:50,370
"The average age of your towers
is 68 years old,

895
00:49:50,370 --> 00:49:53,370
but the mean life expectancy
is only 65."

896
00:49:53,370 --> 00:49:56,170
So, you know, in a sense PG&amp;E
was sort of playing with fire

897
00:49:56,170 --> 00:49:57,430
over the years.

898
00:49:57,430 --> 00:49:58,730
They were basically saying,

899
00:49:58,730 --> 00:50:01,600
"Look, we will let these
transmission lines age in place,

900
00:50:01,600 --> 00:50:03,400
and if there's a problem
with one of them,

901
00:50:03,400 --> 00:50:05,600
we'll go out and fix it."

902
00:50:05,600 --> 00:50:08,270
&gt;&gt; Without climate change,
the consequences of failure

903
00:50:08,270 --> 00:50:11,600
of a transmission line,
it's relatively modest.

904
00:50:11,600 --> 00:50:15,270
It falls down, perhaps, or...
and it causes a fire,

905
00:50:15,270 --> 00:50:18,830
and the fire department comes
and puts it out.

906
00:50:18,830 --> 00:50:21,800
So, the system has been
maintained, you know,

907
00:50:21,800 --> 00:50:24,170
with some preventive maintenance
but also with a philosophy

908
00:50:24,170 --> 00:50:27,070
that it can be run
until it breaks.

909
00:50:27,070 --> 00:50:30,730
The thing is that the costs
have changed.

910
00:50:30,730 --> 00:50:33,470
The risks have changed.

911
00:50:33,470 --> 00:50:36,970
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: PG&amp;E declined to be
interviewed by "Frontline,"

912
00:50:36,970 --> 00:50:39,100
but said in a statement
that the company

913
00:50:39,100 --> 00:50:41,270
"disagrees with any suggestion
that it knew

914
00:50:41,270 --> 00:50:43,500
of any specific maintenance
conditions

915
00:50:43,500 --> 00:50:46,700
that caused the Camp Fire and
nonetheless deferred work

916
00:50:46,700 --> 00:50:50,170
that would have addressed those
conditions."

917
00:50:50,170 --> 00:50:54,270
It added, "Since 2010,
PG&amp;E has spent hundreds

918
00:50:54,270 --> 00:50:58,830
of millions on line
preventative work."

919
00:50:58,830 --> 00:51:01,270
&gt;&gt; PG&amp;E is taking this
extraordinary step of saying,

920
00:51:01,270 --> 00:51:03,970
"Look, we can't handle this
liability anymore.

921
00:51:03,970 --> 00:51:07,470
So that during the days,
red flag days,

922
00:51:07,470 --> 00:51:08,870
when there's low humidity
and high wind,

923
00:51:08,870 --> 00:51:12,070
we're just going to shut off
the power."

924
00:51:12,070 --> 00:51:13,870
And it's sort of a stunning
thing to think about,

925
00:51:13,870 --> 00:51:17,800
but there increasingly,
um, are days,

926
00:51:17,800 --> 00:51:20,500
and-and multiple days
in Northern California,

927
00:51:20,500 --> 00:51:23,970
where communities suddenly
don't have power anymore.

928
00:51:23,970 --> 00:51:26,670
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: PG&amp;E has now filed
for bankruptcy protection

929
00:51:26,670 --> 00:51:30,430
because of liabilities arising
from wildfires.

930
00:51:30,430 --> 00:51:34,230
It estimates that it could face
at least $10.5 billion

931
00:51:34,230 --> 00:51:38,300
in damages from the Camp Fire
alone.

932
00:51:38,300 --> 00:51:40,800
&gt;&gt; I think this is one of the
first real

933
00:51:40,800 --> 00:51:45,870
climate adaptation problems that
at least America has confronted.

934
00:51:47,770 --> 00:51:50,430
And this is not
a static problem.

935
00:51:50,430 --> 00:51:52,570
We have a problem that's going
to grow worse inevitably

936
00:51:52,570 --> 00:51:55,530
over the next several decades.

937
00:51:55,530 --> 00:51:58,070
&gt;&gt; NARRATOR: Some scientists
believe that fires in California

938
00:51:58,070 --> 00:52:00,170
could increase in size
dramatically

939
00:52:00,170 --> 00:52:04,630
by the middle of the century if
temperatures continue to rise.

940
00:52:04,630 --> 00:52:07,030
&gt;&gt; Everything was perfect that
day for a massive,

941
00:52:07,030 --> 00:52:09,600
destructive incident to do what
it did.

942
00:52:09,600 --> 00:52:12,330
And it's in place everywhere.

943
00:52:12,330 --> 00:52:15,170
Everywhere in California,
Arizona, Nevada, Washington,

944
00:52:15,170 --> 00:52:16,570
Oregon.

945
00:52:16,570 --> 00:52:18,130
And it's like what's...

946
00:52:18,130 --> 00:52:20,500
You don't even want to think
about it, like, "What's next?

947
00:52:20,500 --> 00:52:22,230
Can it be worse than that?"

948
00:52:22,230 --> 00:52:23,630
And the answer is yes.

949
00:52:23,630 --> 00:52:27,530
♪ ♪

950
00:52:28,830 --> 00:52:33,570
&gt;&gt; Go to pbs.org/frontline
for more on PG&amp;E.

951
00:52:33,570 --> 00:52:39,030
&gt;&gt; Is what PG&amp;E did-- or did not
do-- grossly negligent?

952
00:52:39,030 --> 00:52:42,300
&gt;&gt; And more about the emergency
alert system in Paradise.

953
00:52:42,300 --> 00:52:44,970
&gt;&gt; People I think they expect
if there's an emergency

954
00:52:44,970 --> 00:52:46,400
they'll get notified.

955
00:52:46,400 --> 00:52:48,030
I think if we did have an order

956
00:52:48,030 --> 00:52:49,730
it would have made a difference
to my mom.

957
00:52:49,730 --> 00:52:51,570
&gt;&gt; Connect to the "Frontline"
community

958
00:52:51,570 --> 00:52:53,230
on Facebook and Twitter,

959
